# Untitled

MOF@lignocellulosic nanofibril aerogel designed by carboxymethylated nanocellulose bridging for thermal insulation and fire retardancy
Zerong Li<sup>1,3</sup> · Zhuoling Chen<sup>1,3</sup> · Qiaoling Huang<sup>1,3</sup> · Song Zhang<sup>1,3</sup> · Wei Wang<sup>2</sup> · Wei Li<sup>1,3</sup>
Abstract
Under the global issues of environmental pollution and energy crisis, cellulose nanofiber (CNF)–based aerogels have attracted great attention for energy-efficient building application. For safety concern, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) could be incorporated to endow aerogels with fire retardancy. However, the aerogels prepared by blending of MOFs with cellulose fibers could not achieve fire resistance, while the in situ growth of MOFs in the system with additional chemicals and even environmental unfriendly processes cause high thermal conductivity of the aerogels. In this work, MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were incorporated to lignocellulosic nanofibril (LCNF) aerogel by carboxymethylated nanocellulose (CMNC) bridging. Such novel binding technique relied on the nanoscaled size and abundant hydroxyl/carboxyl groups of CMNC fibers. In this case, strong molecular interactions were formed between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose fibers to endow aerogel with fire retardancy by char layer formation and gas suppression. In particular, the incorporated MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles provided abundant micropores and somehow prevented the formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonding among fibers to increase the complexity of pore structures. This endowed the aerogel with a thermal conductivity of \(30.6\ \mathrm{mW\ m^{-1}\ K^{-1}}\), which also displayed excellent thermal insulation ability at \(250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(-196\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The green fabrication method would provide a novel idea for the design of MOF/cellulose composites to broaden their applications.
Keywords Lignocellulosic nanofibril aerogel · MIL-53(Al) · Nanocellulose bridging · Thermal insulation · Fire retardancy
## 1 Introduction
Nearly \(40\%\) of the global energy is consumed by air conditioning and heating for building, which has caused global warming and environmental pollution [1]. In order to solve the above-mentioned problems, thermal insulation materials have been developed [2]. Although the commercially available expanded polystyrene foam and extruded polystyrene display excellent thermal insulation, bio-based functional materials for energy-efficient building are highly desirable to carry the lowest carbon footprint [3].
Since the intrinsic complex porous structure of aerogel can reduce heat flow speed, biodegradable aerogels have attracted extensive attention for thermal insulation [4–6]. Cellulose is one of the most abundant natural polymers on the earth, which has the advantages of renewability as well as biodegradability and can be behaved in various forms [7–10]. Additionally, it is reported that the large interfacial surface areas generated by the nanofibrous structure of cellulose nanofibers (CNFs) can behave as phonon barriers to prevent heat conduction. In this case, the pristine CNF aerogels display low thermal conductivity around \(43\ \mathrm{mW\ m^{-1}\ K^{-1}}\) [11, 12]. Even though, the inherent flammability of cellulose fibers may cause fire under elevated temperatures [13].
In order to solve the problem, flame-retardant agents should be incorporated into cellulose-based aerogels. Ren et al. [14] modified CNF aerogel by in situ supramolecular assembly with melamine (MEL) and phytic acid (PA) to achieve fire-resistance properties. However, the organic flame retardants would produce toxic gases during combustion. Although the choosing of inorganic flame
retardants could solve the above-mentioned problem, their dispersion and compatibility in the CNF aerogel should be considered [15].
Metal–organic framework (MOF) is an evolving class of organic–inorganic hybrid materials formed by the coordination between metal ions and organic ligands with high porosity, superior thermal stability and abundant microporosity, which can be applied as thermal insulation and flame retardant materials in theory [16–20]. However, their usage was hindered by the difficulties in processing and molding [21]. In particular, the existing organic moieties of MOF would be beneficial to their compatibility with CNF aerogel. However, Zhou et al. [11] found that CNF-MOF (Al-MIL-53) aerogel prepared by direct blending CNFs and Al-MIL-53 nanoparticles was quickly burnt in fire. Since the dispersed MOF nanoparticles were not able to form interconnected networks with CNFs, they could not protect CNFs under combustion. If CNFs were coated and cross-linked by polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) with Al-MIL-53 nanolayers (content of \(25.6\;\mathrm{wt\%}\)) through in situ growth method, the incorporated MOFs could endow the aerogel with good fire retardancy. Unfortunately, the thermal conductivity of as-fabricated MOF@CNF aerogel \((41\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}})\) displayed no significant change in comparison with that of CNF aerogel \((43\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}})\). Nabipour et al. [22] even pointed out that in situ growth of MOFs (ZIF-8) on the surface of cellulose fibers enhanced the flame retardancy of the aerogel, while its thermal conductivity increased from \(33.5\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}}\) to \(49.7\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}}\) due to the relatively high thermal conductivity of MOFs \((>200\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}})\) [23]. Additionally, although it is reported that the abundant micropores of MOFs could retard gas movements to improve its thermal conductivity [24], this information seems to be ignored in the structure design of MOF-incorporated CNF aerogels.
In order to design MOF@CNF aerogel with both good fire retardancy and low thermal conductivity, in this work, we have proposed a novel strategy for the MOF incorporation to CNF aerogel. In detail, anisotropic MIL-53(Al) \(@\) lignocellulosic nanofibril (LCNF) aerogel was prepared by unidirectional freeze casting and carboxymethylated nanocellulose (CMNC) bridging. Thanks to the nanometer scale and abundant hydroxyl/carboxyl groups of CMNC fibers, MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were successfully integrated into the fiber networks to protect LCNF aerogel under combustion condition. The incorporated MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles also caused complex porous structures in the aerogel, leading to a low thermal conductivity \((30.6\mathrm{~mW~m^{-1}~K^{-1}})\) of the designed aerogel with excellent thermal insulation ability at \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(-196\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2  Experimental section
## 2.1  Materials
Bagasse (measured content of cellulose, hemicellulose and lignin by National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) method: \(45.28\%\) , \(22.13\%\) and \(22.39\%\) ) was obtained from Guangxi Guitang (Guap) Co., Ltd (Guigang, China). Carboxymethylated nanocellulose (CMNC, \(1.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) , diameter: \(4{-}10\ \mathrm{nm}\) , length: \(1{-}3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) , pH: 7–8, carboxyl groups: \(1.2–3.0\ \mathrm{mmol/g}\) , fiber source: eucalyptus) was purchased from Guilin Qihong Technology Co., Ltd (Guilin, China). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH), terephthalic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}\) and aluminum chloride hexahydrate \(\mathrm{(AlCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\) were provided by Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). All the chemicals were used as received.
## 2.2  Preparation of MIL‑53(Al)@CMNC/LCNF aerogels
Bagasse was defibered by a disintegrator (AG 04, Estanit GmbH, Germany) and then ground by using a Superfine grinding mill (1500 rpm, Super Masscolloider MKZA 1015JIV, Masuko Sangyo Co. Ltd, Japan) for five cycles to obtain lignocellulosic nanofibrils (LCNFs). The synthesis of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles was carried out using hydrothermal method as reported in the literatures [25, 26]. In detail, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) \((8.3\ \mathrm{g},\,5\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and NaOH \((4.0\ \mathrm{g},\ 10\ \mathrm{mmol})\) were dissolved in deionized (DI) water \((15\ \mathrm{mL})\) and stirred at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . The mixture was then dropwise added to the \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution \((15\ \mathrm{mL},\ 5\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and reacted at \(105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(18\ \mathrm{h}\) . Finally, the raw product was thoroughly washed by DI water and ethanol followed by drying at \(50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) . The generated MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles \((0,\,25\ \mathrm{mg},\,50\ \mathrm{mg},\,100\ \mathrm{mg},\,250\ \mathrm{mg}\) and \(500\;\mathrm{mg})\) were then separately added into the nanofiber suspension \((1.0\ \mathrm{g},\ \mathrm{dry}\ \mathrm{weight})\) with the CMNC mass percentage of \(20\%\) \(_{(0.2\mathrm{~g)}}\) . The according weight percent of added MOFs was \(0\%\) , \(2.5\%\) , \(5\%\) , \(10\%\) , \(25\%\) , and \(50\%\) to that of the dry weight of the fibers, respectively. After stirring for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\) , the suspension (consistency of \(2\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) ) was then poured into a directional freezing mold (a PMMA box with PDMS bottom) and frozen by liquid nitrogen. Finally, the samples were dried in a lyophilizer for \(48\ \mathrm{h}\) . The resulting CMNC/LCNF aerogels and \(\mathrm{MIL-53(Al)}\ @\) CMNC/LCNF aerogels were named as pristine aerogel, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{2.5}\) , \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{5}\) , \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) , \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{25}\) and \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\alpha})\) \(\mathrm{CLA}_{50}\) , respectively. Additionally, \(\mathrm{MIL-53(Al)}\ @\mathrm{CMNC}\) aerogels \((\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A})\) , \(\mathrm{MIL-53(Al)}\ @\mathrm{LCNF}\) aerogels \((\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\alpha})\) LA) and \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) prepared by in situ growth of MIL-53 (Al) on CMNC fibers \((\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{in-situ}}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10})\) were fabricated for
comparison. The preparation process of \(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{in-situ}}\mathcal{O}\mathrm{CLA}_{10}\) was according to the literature [11]. The specific information of the sample is shown in Table S1.
## 2.3 Characterization
## 2.3.1 Chemical structure
The chemical structure was examined by using an attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (ATR FTIR, Bruker, Germany) in the range of \(4000{-}400\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Lebold \(\mathrm{Max}200\) , the USA). The crystallinity of aerogel and MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Rigaku, Japan) in the scanning range of \(20\!=\!5\!-\!50^{\circ}\) at a scanning rate of \(5^{\circ}\mathrm{\min}^{-1}\) . The zeta potentials of suspensions \((0.01\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) were measured by Malvern Mastersizer (ZS90X, Malvern, UK).
## 2.3.2 Physical properties
The morphology of LCNF and CMNC fibers was observed by transmission electron microscope (TEM, HT7700, Hitachi, Japan). The pore size distribution was measured by automatic mercury injection meter (AutoPore Iv 9510, the USA) and the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas and pore sizes (Micromeritics, ASAP2460, the USA). The morphology was observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, SU1510, Hitachi, Japan) and micro/nano computerized tomography (micro/nano-CT, resolution of \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) , GE Vitomex, the USA). The mechanical properties were evaluated by a universal testing machine (3367U4870, Instron, the USA) with a load of \(500\,\mathrm{N}\) at a rate of \(40\,\mathrm{mm\,min^{-1}}\) during compression tests. The thermogravimetry (TG) curve was measured by a synchronous thermal analyzer (SSTA449 F5, Netzsch, Germany) in the temperature range of \(30{-}600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under a nitrogen atmosphere. The heating rate was \(15\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C\min^{-1}}\) with a shielding gas flow rate of \(40\,\mathrm{mL\,min^{-1}}\) .
## 2.3.3 Thermal insulation and fire retardancy
The thermal conductivity was measured by a transient thermal plane conductivity meter (TPS 2500S, Hot Disk, Sweden) at room temperature (RT) under a relative humidity (RH) of \(35\%\) . Samples were incubated at the test temperature for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) before measurement. The infrared thermal imaging camera (FLIR E4, the USA) was used to record their surface temperatures. The fire resistance was tested by burning with alcohol lamp \((\sim\!500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . Heat release capacity was measured by miniature calorimeter (MCC, FFT-0001, Britain). According to ISO 5660–1, the flammability of the samples \((10\times10\times3~\mathrm{mm}^{3})\) was characterized by cone calorimeter (Fire Testing Technology, UK) at heat flux of
\(50\,\mathrm{kW\,m}^{-2}\) . The sound absorption coefficient was tested by transfer function sound absorption coefficient test system (B.K 4206, BSWA, China).
## 2.3.4 Sound absorption
The sound absorption coefficient \((\alpha)\) of sample (diameter: \(29\;\mathrm{mm}\) , height: \(30\;\mathrm{mm}\) ) was measured by transfer function sound absorption coefficient test system (B.K 4206, BSWA, China) at a frequency of \(500{-}6000\ \mathrm{Hz}\) according to GB/T 18696.2–2002, which was calculated by formula (1) [27]:
\[
\alpha=1-|r|^2=1-rr^2-ri^2
\]
where, r, \(\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{r}}\) and \(\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{i}}\) are normal incidence sound pressure reflection factor, the real part of reflection factor, and the imaginary part of reflection factor, respectively.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Chemical structure
The MIL-53(Al) \(@\) CMNC/LCNF aerogel \((\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{CLA})\) was facilely prepared by the mixing of LCNFs, CMNC and MIL-53(Al) followed by unidirectional freeze casting and freeze-drying (Fig. 1a). The diameter of LCNF and CMNC fibers was in \(10{-}90\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(4{-}10\;\mathrm{nm}\) , respectively (Fig. S1). The chosen of LCNFs would simplify the preparation process, avoid additional chemical consumption and make full use of lignocellulose components. However, without the addition of CMNC fibers, the aerogel underwent collapse due to the limited molecular interaction among LCNFs that resulted from the residual lignin in the fibers as well as the weak molecular interaction between LCNFs and MIL-53(Al) (Fig. S2a). In detail, when CMNC fibers were incorporated into the MIL-53(Al)/LCNFs suspension, they not only mechanically entangled and formed hydrogen bonding with LCNFs, but also interacted with MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles by electrostatic adsorption and coordination bonding due to their abundant hydroxyl/carboxyl groups and nanoscaled size [28, 29]. The resulting aerogel was ultra-light \((21.0{-}35.1\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) and shapeable (Fig. 1b–c). Interestingly, the shape of the aerogel almost kept constant after storage for 30 days in ambient environment (temperature of \(25\pm1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and relative humidity of \(50\pm5\%\) , Fig. 1d), which could be due to the hydrophobicity of lignin in the CNFs [30–32]. Additionally, although MIL-53 \(@\) CMNC aerogel could be formed, it displayed obvious shrink under the same condition due to the hydrophilicity of CMNC fibers (Fig. S2b).
In order to explore the molecular interaction in \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}\) CLA, its chemical composition was investigated by FTIR
and XPS in comparison with that of the pristine aerogel. As shown in Fig. 2a, the peaks for \(\mathbf{C}\!=\!\mathbf{O}\) , aromatic \(\mathbf{C}\!=\!\mathbf{C}\) , C-O and aromatic \(\mathrm{C-H}\) groups in the pristine aerogel were clearly observed at 1731, 1600, 1514, 1420 and \(754~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , respectively [33–35]. When the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticle contents were 2.5 and \(5~\mathrm{wt\%}\) , the chemical structure of aerogels almost unchanged. While, at a MIL-53(Al) dosage of \(10\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) , the intensity of the above-mentioned peaks enhanced in \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) due to peak overlapping. Additionally, new peak for O–Al-O group appeared at \(594~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [36]. Meanwhile, the -OH groups of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10-50}\) shifted from 3400 to \(3339~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) with weaker intensity and wider peak area, indicating that hydrogen bonds were formed between hydroxyl/carboxyl groups in cellulose fibers and carboxyl groups in terephthalic acid of MOFs (Fig. 2b). The peak of carboxyl groups also red shifted from 1731 to \(1700\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) when the content of MOF increased to more than \(10\%\) (Fig. 2c). These phenomena can be attributed to the coordination interaction between the metal nodes in MIL53(Al) nanoparticles and hydroxyl/carboxyl groups in cellulose fibers [37, 38].
Similarly, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) displayed stronger peaks of C-H/C \((284.6~\mathrm{eV})\) and \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) \((73.9\;\mathrm{eV})\) as well as weaker peaks of C–O–C \((287.7~\mathrm{eV})\) in the XPS spectra (Fig. 2d–e) [38]. Furthermore, obvious characteristic diffraction peaks of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles appeared in the \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10-50}\) at 2θ of \(9.21^{\circ}\) (101), \(10.5^{\circ}\) (200), \(15.37^{\circ}\) (011) and \(18.44^{\circ}\) (211) (Fig. 2f) [39–41]. Due to the increase of MIL-53(Al) content, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{50}\) has more chaotic peaks than that at a low MOF concentration. All the above results indicated that MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were successfully incorporated into \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10-50}\) with abundant molecular interactions in the system. In addition, it should be mentioned that the aerogel prepared without the incorporation of CMNC \((\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A})\) displayed no obvious difference in the chemical structure in comparison with that of the pristine aerogel, indicating the weak molecular interaction between LCNFs and MIL53(Al) nanoparticles (Fig. S3a–c). This phenomenon can be attributed to that the more negatively charged CMNC fibers \((-44.20~\mathrm{mV})\) in comparison with that of LCNFs \((-6.14~\mathrm{mV})\) (Fig. S4a) could form a stronger electrostatic interaction with the \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) sites in MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles
\((20.76\;\mathrm{mV})\) [42]. With the increase in MIL-53(Al) content, the zeta potential of MIL-53(Al) \(@\) CMNC/LCNFs suspension decreased from \(-31.5\,\mathrm{m}\mathrm{-}14.4\,\mathrm{mV}\) (Fig. S4b). According to the literature, nanocellulose suspensions with zeta potential range from 25 to \(-\,25~\mathrm{mV}\) tended to agglomerate [43]. This meant that the suspensions of \(\mathrm{M@CLA}_{2.5-25}\) were relatively stable while suspensions of \(\mathrm{M@CLA}_{50}\) tended to be aggregated.
To further clarify the molecular interaction, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were used to elucidate the binding behavior between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose dimer before and after carboxymethylation. Detailed calculation methods could be found in the supporting information. In the simulations, cellulose dimer was placed nearby the MIL-53(Al), and the interactions and binding energy were calculated after the structure optimization. As shown in Fig. 2g, the binding energy between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose dimer was 540 and \(886\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) before and after carboxymethylation, respectively. This result indicated that CMNC fibers could form a stronger molecular interaction with MIL-53(Al), which would play a key role in the properties of \(\mathrm{M@CLA}\).
optimization. As shown in Fig. 2g, the binding energy between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose dimer was 540 and \(886\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) before and after carboxymethylation, respectively. This result indicated that CMNC fibers could form a stronger molecular interaction with MIL-53(Al), which would play a key role in the properties of \(\mathrm{M@CLA}\) .
## 3.2  Microstructure
The internal microstructure of aerogels was observed by micro/nano-CT and SEM. Note that, the white areas represent the skeleton of aerogel, while the black ones represent the pores in micro/nano-CT images. The \(\mathrm{M@CLA}\) showed well-aligned lamellar structure with high anisotropy (Figs. 3a–d and S5). This phenomenon could be explained by that ice crystals vertically grew due to the unidirectional temperature
difference in the freezing process [44]. Furthermore, the lamella of pristine aerogel was thick with a narrow space in between, which could be attributed to the agglomeration of cellulose nanofibers (Fig. 3b1). After the incorporation of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles, the lamellar structure of \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) \(\mathrm{CLA_{10}}\) became thin and uniform with a layer space of approximately \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Fig. 3c1). With the increase in the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles content, the inter-lamellar space of the aerogels decreased after initial increase (Figs. 3b–d and S6a1–c1). Under a magnified condition, obvious MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles appeared on the surface of \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) (Fig. 3b2–d2). In particular, the Al elements evenly distributed in the aerogel, indicating that the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were successfully introduced to the porous network structure (insert images of Figs. 3c2–d2 and S6b). Additionally, the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles tended to aggregate with their increased amount due to its nanostructure (Fig. S7). Furthermore, it should be noted that \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) showed a dense structure without pores, and micron-scaled particles can be observed on the surface of aerogel. In comparison with that of \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) , its Al elements content significantly decreased (Fig. S6d). This also indicated the CMNC fibers behaved as bridges between LCNFs and MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles to support the skeleton structure of aerogel with more MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles retention.
To further investigate the effect of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles content on the microstructure of aerogels, their pore distributions were measured by mercury injection meter. The pristine aerogel showed an obvious second-order pore size distribution (Fig. 3e). After the introduction of MIL-53(Al), the number of pores with size of \(102.1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) in \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) CLA significantly reduced, while the pore size increased from \(7.2\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (pristine aerogel) to \(11.5\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) \((\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{CLA}_{10})\) and then decreased to \(10.1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) \((\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{CLA}_{50})\) with the increase in the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticle dosage. This phenomenon could be attributed to that MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles partially hampered the formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonding among nanocellulose fibers, which partitioned the porous structures of aerogel [45]. This led to the homogeneity of the pore size and increase in the complexity of lamellar structures in \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) CLA. The microstructures of pristine aerogel, \(\mathrm{M@CLA_{10}}\) and \(\mathrm{M@CLA}_{50}\) are schematically illustrated in Fig. 3f. Additionally, the introduction of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles could also endow aerogel with abundant micropores, which enriched the pore structures of aerogel (Fig. S6f–g). Furthermore, their density and porosity displayed the similar normal distribution (Table S2). The \(\mathrm{M@CLA_{10}}\) exhibited the lowest density and highest porosity with a value of \(21.0\pm0.1~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-3}\) and \(98.6\%\) , respectively.
This result also suggested that MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles introduced networks with cellulose fibers by molecular interactions to enrich, support and partition porous structure of the aerogel rather than physical deposition. When the dosage of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles further increased, the porosity of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) decreased due to the aggregation of nanoparticles.
## 3.3 Mechanical properties
The mechanical properties of aerogels are of great significance to their stability and recyclability in the practical application. The \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{CLA}_{10}\) showed anisotropic compressive stress of \(27.3\pm0.3\;\mathrm{kPa}\) and \(4.9\pm0.1\;\mathrm{kPa}\) in the axial and radial directions due to the highly ordered microstructures, respectively (Fig. 4a–b). In the axial direction, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) could support a weight of 1000 times of its own weight (Fig. 4d). Its stress–strain curve translated from the linear elastic region to the plastic deformation region at \(10\%\) strain, and the height retention rate was \(77.8\%\) at \(40\%\) strain after 10 cycles. In contrast, the aerogel displayed elastic
deformation in the radial direction. The microstructure and lamellar distance of aerogel still kept stable, and its height retention rate increased to \(96.0\%\) under the same conditions (Figs. 4e and S8). The slight decrease in the elasticity could be attributed to the buckling and breaking of the interconnected submicron fibers [46]. The anisotropic compressive stress of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) could be explained as follows: on the one hand, the vertical lamellar structure was difficult to be bent in the axial direction, leading to a stronger compressive strength; on the other hand, the flexibility in the radial direction increased by continuous and parallel wave-shaped microstructure. Additionally, the compressive stress of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) increased with the increase in the strain (Fig. 4c). At a strain of \(80\%\) , its compression stress was \(47\;\mathrm{kPa}\).
In the following, the effect of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticle content on the compressive strength of aerogels was investigated. It can be seen from Fig. 4f that the compression strength increased with the rising of MIL-53(Al) nanoparticle content. This result could be explained by that MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles were integrated into the fiber networks by CMNC bridging, leading to the enhancement
of the lamellar structures in aerogel. Furthermore, at a MIL-53(Al) content of \(25{-}50\;\mathrm{wt}\%\), the structure started to densify by physically adsorbed nanoparticles filling in the network structure of aerogels after \(20\%\) strain, leading to an improved mechanical strength (Fig. 4g). It is reported that the mechanical properties of CNF-based aerogels seem to mainly depend on the fiber physical entanglement and molecular interaction [6]. Since the existence of lignin in LCNFs would not only increase the diameter and stiffness of cellulose nanofibers to affect the fiber entangling [12], but also hinder the molecular interactions among cellulose fibers as well as with MOFs, the preparation of LCNFs with more polar groups is carried on in our group to further enhance the mechanical strength of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\).
## 3.4  Application
Since MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles have been successfully integrated into cellulose networks through CMNC bridging and enriched the pore structure of the resulting aerogel, the possibility to adjust the balance between fire retardancy and thermal conductivity of aerogel needs to be explored. As displayed in Figs. 5a and S9, the temperature at the top of \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{CLA}_{10}\) ( \(1.5\,\mathrm{cm}\) above) increased from 40.3 to \(50.2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) or from 36.3 to \(43.4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) on a heating stage at \(125\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), respectively. It decreased from 24.8 to \(21.6\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) on copper plates exposed at liquid nitrogen, respectively. Such
thermal insulation behaviors can be ascribed to that heat was restricted in the bottom of aerogels via two heat transfer mechanisms. On the one hand, the low density and high porosity of aerogels effectively diminished solid conductivity. On the other hand, the anisotropic structure of aerogel resisted the air flow, which hampered the heat transfer via air convection. Furthermore, the finger area under the aerogel almost "disappeared" in the thermal imaging once covered with \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) (Fig. 5b), indicating that \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) could be applied for thermal stealth materials.
In order to quantify the thermal insulation properties of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\), the thermal conductivity was evaluated. It can be seen from Fig. 5c that the thermal conductivities of pristine aerogels and \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{2.5-50}\) were 43.7, 36.8, 35.5, 30.6, 36.4 and \(45.2\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{K}^{-1}\), respectively. With the increase in the MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles content, the thermal conductivities of the aerogels displayed initial decrease and then increase. This phenomenon could be explained as follows: on the one hand, the molecular interaction between cellulose fibers and MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles reflected and scattered phonons; on the other hand, the thermal conduction of air \(\left(\lambda_{\mathrm{air}}\right)\) was further limited by the mesopores \((\sim18\;\mathrm{nm})\) of the MIL-53(Al) in the aerogel, which were significantly smaller than the mean free path of the main molecules \((\mathbf{N}_{2},\,\mathbf{O}_{2})\) in the air ( \(70\;\mathrm{nm}\) under ambient conditions) [47]. However, at a MIL-53(Al) dosage of \(25\%\) and \(50\%\), their aggregation caused a decrease in thermal insulation due to the intrinsic
high thermal conductivity of MOFs [48]. The optimal thermal conductivity of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) was lower than the recently published bio-based aerogels (Table 1) and \(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{in-situ}}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) \((44.0\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{K}^{-1})\) . This indicated that MOF@LCNF aerogel prepared by the in situ growth of MIL-53(Al) on cellulose fibers caused higher thermal conductivity, which was consistent with the literatures [11, 22]. Additionally, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) displayed anisotropic thermal conductivities due to anisotropic structure (axial direction: \(45.4\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{K}^{-1}\) ; radial direction: \(30.6\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{K}^{-1}\) , Fig. 5d). This result could be attributed to that continuous and parallel wave-shaped lamellas of \(\mathbf{M}(\boldsymbol{\omega}\) CLA inhibited the thermal localization and reduced heat flow speed in the radial direction (Insert images in Fig. 5e) [49]. In this case, the speed of heat flow could be reduced, leading to a low thermal conductivity. The thermal insulation ability of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) was better than that of commercial foams and previous studies (Table 1).
Furthermore, fire retardancy is beneficial to the safety of thermal insulation materials under evaluated temperatures. As shown in Figs. 6a and S10, the pristine aerogel and \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) completely ignited and rapidly burned within \(2\;\mathrm{s}\) . During the combustion process, the peak heat release rate (PHRR) and total heat release (THR) of pristine aerogel were \(190.1\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) and \(5.6\;\mathrm{mJ}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) , respectively. In contrast, \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) only exhibited smoldering during the combustion by an alcohol lamp ignition test \((\sim500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , and PHRR and THR decreased to \(116.3\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) and \(4.0\;\mathrm{mJ}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) , respectively (Fig. 6b–c and Table 2). Micro-calorimeter (MCC) test also showed the similar heat release results (Table S3 and Fig. S11), indicating the molecular interactions between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose fibers endowed aerogel with better fire retardancy. The flame retardancy of
aerogel can be explained by microporosity and high thermal stability of MIL-53(Al), and molecular interactions between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose fibers [55]. Additionally, MIL-53(Al) could form protective char layer to suppress combustion after flaming by the generation of metal oxide on the polymer surface [56]. Furthermore, it is well established that smoke and toxic gases are predominant hazard factors in fire accident. As shown in Fig. S12, the peak smoke production rate (PSPR), total smoke production (TSP), the CO production rate (COP) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) production rate \((\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{P})\) were \(0.01\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , \(0.22\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\) , 0.0041 and \(0.076\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , respectively, which were lower than reported datas. This could be explained by that micro-pore structure of MOF suppresses the gases release during combustion.
In the next, the thermal stability of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) was investigated. As shown in the TGA (Fig. 6d) and DTG curves (Fig. S13), the onset temperature of the maximum mass loss was almost the same in the pristine aerogel and \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) , and an endothermic peak appeared at \(318.3\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with the maximum pyrolysis rate of \(9.71\%\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) in the pristine aerogel, which could be attributed to the thermal decomposition of lignocellulosic fibers. Such pyrolysis rate decreased to \(4.95\%\) \(\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) in the \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) , indicating the thermal protective effect of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}\) by the molecular interactions between fibers and MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles. Additionally, another endothermic peak appeared in the \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) in the temperature range from 310.5 to \(383.0\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , which partially overlapped with the endothermic peak of the pristine aerogel. This might be resulted from the gradual thermal degradation of the fiber/ MIL-53(Al) complex and MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles [63]. This result could be explained by that MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles could not form interconnected networks structure with cellulose fibers without CMNC bridging. Additionally, an obvious decomposition of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) happened at \(574\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , which could be due to the thermal decomposition of MIL53(Al) nanoparticles to form aluminum oxide. The oxide could support the fiber network of aerogel under combustion. Based on the above discussion, the flame resistance mechanism of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) was summarized in Fig. 6f. Since the thermal decomposition of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) started at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , its long-term performance at that temperature was investigated. As depicted in Fig. S14a–f, during a 10-h heating period, the temperature at the top of \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) increased from \(41.9\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to approximately \(71.2\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . In particular, although the aerogel displayed thermal degradation at around \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , its shape almost kept intact with partial carbonization on its heating face throughout this process, which could be attributed to the heat consumption by its open application atmosphere (Fig. S14g–h). These results suggested that \(\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{A}_{10}\) might be applied at elevated temperatures for a certain time.
Additionally, since noise pollution has become increasingly serious due to the accelerated development of society, a quiet environment is also desirable for energy-efficient
building to guarantee our physical and mental health [64–67]. \(\mathrm{M@CLA_{10}}\) displayed an excellent sound-absorption coefficient \((\sim\!0.9)\) in the \(1600{-}6300\ \mathrm{Hz}\) at high frequencies, which was much better than that of the pristine
aerogel (Fig. 6e). This could be ascribed to that the complex porous structure in \(\mathbf{M}@\) CLA increased the diffuse refraction or reflection of sound waves, and the microspores of incorporated MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles further increased the
friction and air viscosity consumption [68]. In conclusion, the flame retardancy, excellent thermal and sound insulation abilities endowed our designed aerogel with great application perspective for energy-efficient building.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, MIL-53(Al)@CMNC/LCNF aerogels (M@ CLA) with anisotropic structure was prepared by unidirectional freezing. Based on the abundant hydroxyl/carboxyl groups and nanoscaled size, CMNC fibers tended to behave as bridges between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and LCNFs to assemble them together. The molecular interactions between MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles and cellulose fibers endowed aerogel with fire retardancy and limited toxic gas production. Additionally, the incorporated MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles enhanced the lamellar structures and enriched the pore structures of aerogel, leading to \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{CLA}_{10}\) with low density \((21.0~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-3})\), high porosity \((98.6\%)\), low thermal conductivity \((30.6\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{K}^{-1})\) and high sound absorption coefficient \((\sim\!0.9,1600{-}6300\,\mathrm{Hz})\). The obtained aerogel displayed great application prospect for energy-efficiency building. The design idea in this research would be a good guideline for the development of other MOF-functionalized bio-based aerogels.
